];

const faqItems = [
  {
    q: 'What are career development skills?',
    a: 'They are the skills that move your career forward, not only the skills you need to do today\'s tasks. In practice they fall into three groups: a core skill for your field, a multiplier skill that raises the value of that core skill (for example data, sales sense or AI-assisted workflow), and everyday professional habits such as clear writing and self-management. The right mix depends on what is currently holding your career back.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which career development skill should I learn first?',
    a: 'Start from the blocker, not the trend. If you are not getting shortlisted, build one visible, role-specific sample. If you get interviews but no offers, practise explaining real decisions and go deeper in one tool. If you are employed but not growing, take on a reporting, process or project task. If your pay is capped, look at a multiplier such as negotiation, analytics or a domain-plus-AI skill.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How many skills should I work on at one time?',
    a: 'One main skill and, if you have the energy, one small supporting habit such as weekly written updates. Learning several skills at once usually leaves you with shallow notes on all of them and proof of none. Finish one visible piece of work, then decide what to add.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Are soft skills or technical skills more important for career growth?',
    a: 'Neither works alone. Technical or domain skill gets you shortlisted and trusted with tasks. Communication, judgment and stakeholder handling decide how far the same skill takes you. Global employer surveys, including the World Economic Forum\'s, rank analytical thinking, resilience and leadership alongside AI and data skills for exactly this reason.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do certificates count as proof of a career development skill?',
    a: 'Sometimes, but rarely on their own. A certificate is stronger when the credential is specifically required or respected for the role. For most skills, a finished piece of work, a documented result or feedback from someone working in the field is stronger. Use the certificate as support for the work, not as a replacement for it.',
  },
  {
    q: 'I work full time. How can I build a career development skill without burning out?',
    a: 'Plan around a small weekly block you can repeat, sized to your real week, rather than a heroic schedule that collapses after a few days. Aim the learning at something you can use on your current job, so the work itself becomes your practice and your proof. Some people need a few weeks to feel traction, others a couple of months. Both are normal.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Are there free government options for building skills in India?',
    a: 'Yes. Skill India Digital Hub lists free courses. SWAYAM and NPTEL offer free course content, with a paid proctored exam if you want the certificate. The AICTE National Internship Portal is free to register on and lists verified internships for eligible students. Confirm the current eligibility, fees and credit rules on each official site before you rely on them.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does a degree matter for career development skills?',
    a: 'A degree still matters where it is a legal or practical requirement, such as medicine, law or licensed engineering roles. For many other paths, employers increasingly look at what you can show you can do. The useful position is to keep the degree you have or need, and build a skill portfolio alongside it instead of relying on the degree alone.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How is this different from a skill assessment test?',
    a: 'A skill assessment gives you a readiness and role-fit signal about where you stand. This page helps you decide which skill to build once you know your blocker. Many people find it useful to take the assessment first, then use the blocker check to choose what to learn.',
